menu "Contact sensor (reed switch)"

    config CONTACT_SENSOR_GPIO
        int "Reed switch GPIO"
        range 0 27
        default 4
        help
            GPIO wired to one side of the reed switch; the other side goes to GND. The internal pull-up of this
            GPIO is enabled, no external resistor is needed for short wires.

            GPIO4 is only a suggestion: check the pinout printed on your ESP32-H2 Mini board. It is not a
            strapping pin. Do not use GPIO6-7 and GPIO15-21 (in-package flash, not available on the module pins)
            or GPIO26-27 (USB Serial/JTAG: flashing and logs); the build stops with an error if you do. Avoid
            the strapping pins GPIO2, GPIO3, GPIO8, GPIO9 and GPIO25: a closed contact on them at power-up can
            change the boot mode. GPIO23/GPIO24 are the UART0 console pins.

    config CONTACT_ACTIVE_LOW
        bool "Contact closed (door closed) reads LOW"
        default y
        help
            y (default): a normally-open reed switch to GND. Magnet at the switch (door closed) closes the
            contact and pulls the GPIO LOW; magnet away (door open) leaves the pull-up: HIGH.
              GPIO LOW  = door closed
              GPIO HIGH = door open

            n: the opposite, for a normally-closed reed switch or a module with an inverted output:
              GPIO HIGH = door closed
              GPIO LOW  = door open

    config CONTACT_DEBOUNCE_MS
        int "Debounce time (ms)"
        range 10 500
        default 50
        help
            The contact state is accepted only after the GPIO has been quiet (no edge) for this time. Every new
            edge restarts the wait, so contact bounce and vibration are filtered out. Reed switches bounce for
            well under 5 ms; 50 ms also absorbs a door that rattles in its frame while closing. Larger values
            delay the report by the same amount.

endmenu

menu "Factory reset button"

    config FACTORY_RESET_GPIO
        int "Factory reset button GPIO"
        range 0 27
        default 9
        help
            Push button between this GPIO and GND (internal pull-up). Default GPIO9 is the BOOT button of most
            ESP32-H2 boards, but check your board: GPIO9 is a strapping pin. Holding it while the board powers up
            or resets starts the ROM download mode (no firmware runs, so no factory reset happens): press it only
            after the firmware has started.

            Must differ from the reed switch and LED pins; GPIO6-7, GPIO15-21 and GPIO26-27 are rejected at build
            time (see "Reed switch GPIO").

    config FACTORY_RESET_HOLD_MS
        int "Hold time for factory reset (ms)"
        range 3000 30000
        default 6000
        help
            Holding the button this long erases all Matter fabrics and the Thread network credentials, then
            restarts the device so it can be commissioned again. Shorter presses do nothing.

endmenu

menu "Status LED"

    config STATUS_LED_ENABLE
        bool "Status LED on a GPIO"
        default n
        help
            Optional single-color LED (with a series resistor) on a GPIO. ESP32-H2 Mini boards do not share a
            common LED pin, and boards whose LED is an addressable RGB LED (WS2812 type, often on GPIO8) cannot be
            driven as a plain GPIO LED: in that case wire an external LED to a free GPIO.

            Patterns: solid on = factory reset running; fast blink = reset button held; medium blink = Identify;
            slow blink = commissioning window open; off otherwise (or the door state, see below).

    config STATUS_LED_GPIO
        int "Status LED GPIO"
        depends on STATUS_LED_ENABLE
        range 0 27
        default 5
        help
            GPIO of the LED. GPIO5 is only a placeholder: set the pin your LED is actually wired to. Must differ
            from the reed switch and button pins.

    config STATUS_LED_ACTIVE_HIGH
        bool "LED lights when the GPIO is HIGH"
        depends on STATUS_LED_ENABLE
        default y
        help
            y: LED from the GPIO through a resistor to GND. n: LED from 3V3 to the GPIO (lights on LOW).

    config STATUS_LED_SHOW_CONTACT
        bool "LED on while the door is open"
        depends on STATUS_LED_ENABLE
        default n
        help
            When no other pattern is active, light the LED while the contact is open. Useful to check the
            wiring and the debounce on the bench.

endmenu

menu "Matter onboarding (development)"

    config CONTACT_PRINT_ONBOARDING_CODES
        bool "Print the QR code and manual pairing code on the serial console"
        default y
        help
            While the device is not commissioned, print the onboarding payload (QR code text, a URL that shows
            the QR code, and the 11-digit manual pairing code) after start-up. With the default test credentials
            these are the well-known development codes (passcode 20202021, discriminator 3840). With factory
            data from esp-matter-mfg-tool the passcode is not stored on the device, so nothing can be printed:
            use the codes printed by esp-matter-mfg-tool instead.

endmenu
